  "scope": "# Review: 66e63986855b\nTask source is generator `4.0.0`, SHA-256 `8f78ef0944e40e80f9b86e9d5fdb2c9515fd05240e400d097e9993c5af79bc2f`, rule hash `e9ecd02cbe2e`.\nMissing parametric evidence \u2192 built an isolated episode-wide prototype; the approved production generator was not changed.\nThe prototype varies `fill_extent \u2208 {reflected, to_axis}` and `source_policy \u2208 {clear, retain}` while preserving the row-front reasoning pattern.\nThe canonical/default `reflected-clear` program is byte-identical to the canonical Racket source, SHA-256 `e228483658ada007f41f36b034b9aab19ff06535e4fdec54757c831d386421b5`.\nFour retained episodes cover all four settings; each has three demonstrations and four distinct Racket-labelled queries.\nAll 16 queries reject every answer-changing declared rival using demonstrations only; Python/Racket parity passes on all 28 retained pairs.\nFocused prototype tests pass `10/10`; the complete selected-task suite passes `44/44`.\nThe adversarial pass confirms bounded sampler exhaustion, visible-payload deduplication, sorted batch IDs, atomic two-directory rollback, content-addressed source exports, public-ID invariance to hidden metadata/query answers, and sensitivity to visible inputs.\nReference-rule changes: none. Production generation, canonical data, generator approval, variant approval, and training admission remain unchanged.\nLimits: four seeds, medium-band selected-generator inputs, two binary parameters, and the declared rivals only; this is not universal identifiability evidence.\nVerdict: routine prototype handoff; needs attention only before any promotion because all noncanonical settings remain proposed and unapproved.",
